# Releases

## Versioning

Bricola uses [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/) (`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`). The app is private (not an npm package); versions are for tags, GitHub Releases, and `package.json`.

| Bump | When |
|------|------|
| **MAJOR** | Deliberate shipped-product cut (`1.0.0`), or later breaking user/data changes |
| **MINOR** | New user-visible capability |
| **PATCH** | Fixes and small tweaks with no new capability |

After the first shipped-product release (`1.0.0`), bump **MINOR** for new capability, **PATCH** for fixes, and **MAJOR** only for breaking user/data changes.

## Branch model

- Day-to-day: feature PRs into **`dev`** (see pull-requests rule).
- Release: when a batch is ready, cut from current `dev`, open a PR into **`main`**, merge, then tag on **`main`**.
- Tag only commits on **`main`**. Never tag from `dev`.

### Release PR head (do not use `dev`)

Prefer a short-lived **`release/X.Y.Z`** branch from `dev` as the PR head into `main` — not `dev` itself.

```text
git fetch origin
git checkout -b release/X.Y.Z origin/dev
# bump version + CHANGELOG if not already on dev, push, open PR → main
```

**Why:** GitHub’s “Automatically delete head branches” deletes the PR’s **source** branch after merge. Using `dev` as head risks deleting the long-lived integration branch (unless `dev` is the repo default, which GitHub will not auto-delete — still don’t rely on that). A `release/X.Y.Z` head is safe to delete.

### Merge strategy

Use a normal **merge commit** for release PRs into `main` (not squash). Preserves the feature history already on `dev` and keeps the follow-up `main` → `dev` sync clean. Squash remains fine for feature PRs into `dev`.

## Artifacts per release

1. Branch `release/X.Y.Z` from `dev` (or bump on `dev` first, then branch).
2. Bump `package.json` / `package-lock.json` to `X.Y.Z` if not already done.
3. Prepend a `## X.Y.Z` section to `CHANGELOG.md` (user-facing notes).
4. Open PR **`release/X.Y.Z` → `main`**; merge with a **merge commit**.
5. Annotated tag on the merge commit: `git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m "vX.Y.Z"` then `git push origin vX.Y.Z`.
6. GitHub Release from that tag (`gh release create vX.Y.Z …`) using the CHANGELOG notes.
7. Merge `main` back into `dev` if needed so versions do not diverge.

## Checklist

```text
[ ] Decide MAJOR / MINOR / PATCH from changes since last tag
[ ] Branch release/X.Y.Z from dev (do not PR from dev itself)
[ ] Bump package.json (+ lockfile) to X.Y.Z
[ ] Write CHANGELOG.md section for X.Y.Z
[ ] Merge release PR into main (merge commit, not squash)
[ ] Tag vX.Y.Z on the merge commit on main
[ ] Push tag; create GitHub Release with notes
[ ] Confirm production deploy tracks main (if applicable)
[ ] Merge main back to dev if needed so versions don’t diverge
```

## Command recipe (next release)

Replace `X.Y.Z` throughout. Ask for the user’s PR author paragraph before creating the PR (see pull-requests rule).

```bash
# Version + notes on dev (or on the release branch)
git fetch origin
git checkout dev && git pull
# bump package.json / package-lock.json; prepend ## X.Y.Z to CHANGELOG.md
git add package.json package-lock.json CHANGELOG.md
git commit -m "Bump to X.Y.Z and document the release."
git push origin dev

# Release branch (safe head if auto-delete is on)
git checkout -b release/X.Y.Z origin/dev
git push -u origin HEAD

# PR → main (paste user’s author paragraph at the top of the body)
gh pr create --base main --head release/X.Y.Z --title "Release X.Y.Z" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
<author paragraph>

## Summary

- …

## Test plan

- [ ] …

EOF
)"

# Merge with a merge commit (not squash)
gh pr merge --merge

# Tag + GitHub Release on main
git fetch origin main
git checkout main && git pull
git tag -a vX.Y.Z -m "vX.Y.Z"
git push origin vX.Y.Z
gh release create vX.Y.Z --title "vX.Y.Z" --notes "$(sed -n '/^## X.Y.Z/,/^## /{ /^## X.Y.Z/d; /^## /d; p; }' CHANGELOG.md)"

# Sync main → dev
git checkout dev && git pull
git merge origin/main
git push origin dev
```

## Hotfixes

Branch from the release tag or from `main`, ship a **PATCH**, tag + GitHub Release, then merge back into `dev`.
